Title: "Cheatsheet"
Id: "ac7712fc-0a1b-4e7a-8be7-66ed68156f54"

Text: "This page summarizes the syntax implemented by the current Rust code. For the full rule list, see Current Implementation Notes."
Id: "bb702f3d-ec0b-4fac-96a3-08620bf4b2ec"

SectionTitle: "Page Content"
Id: "9ee50d16-4081-4565-a589-e17d0883a854"

Text: "Top-level prose blocks render directly on the page, not inside cards.

```text
Title: \"A Page Title\"
SectionTitle: \"A Section\"
SubsectionTitle: \"A Subsection\"
Text: \"Markdown prose with $x \\in X$ and display math.\"
```

`Text:` supports Markdown and LaTeX. Quoted text fields may span multiple source lines."
Id: "e04dde63-6571-46ad-a6a9-e59f61bec5bd"

SectionTitle: "Commands And Builtins"
Id: "91d6d84b-e6af-46c4-bf1b-78862847d790"

Text: "Commands start with one backslash. Builtin commands and builtin types start with two backslashes.

```text
\\set
\\function:on{A}:to{B}
\\function:on{A}:?to{B}        -- optional tail part, declared in a header
x \\.set.union./ y             -- infix command
A \\:subset:/ B                -- infix specification command
\\(injective)::function        -- refined command type
\\set@{x_ : x_ is \\real}       -- soft build
\\set@!m                       -- hard build
```

The builtin types are `\\\\type`, `\\\\statement`, `\\\\expression`, `\\\\specification`, `\\\\opaque`, and `\\\\abstract`. `\\\\opaque` is satisfied by any declared value, but it does not establish any more specific type information.

Builtin clause commands are expression forms, with semicolon-separated arguments:

```text
\\\\not{P}
\\\\and{P; Q}
\\\\or{P; Q}
\\\\exists{x, y is \\real; n is \\natural}:suchThat{x * n > y}
\\\\existsUnique{x is \\real}
\\\\forAll{x is \\set}:then{x is? \\set}
\\\\if{P}:then{Q}
\\\\have{P}:iff{Q}
\\\\given{x is \\set}:then{P}
\\\\piecewise:if{x = 0}:then{y = 0}:else{y = 1}
```

The variadic builtins map and reduce over slices:

```text
\\\\map{x[1...i_...n]}:to{x[i_] + 1}
\\\\leftReduce{`+`}:on{x[1...n]}
```"
Id: "9d080835-77dd-4347-9bb0-23f3ae05266c"

SectionTitle: "Expressions"
Id: "519c8cf9-e23b-462c-b070-7bd85dc09bbc"

Text: "```text
x * y                -- plain operator, resolved locally or globally
x :* y               -- resolve operator from the left operand type
x *: y               -- resolve operator from the right operand type
x :*: y              -- resolve operator from the common ancestor type

x |op| y             -- named infix operator, same as op(x, y)
x |M.*| y            -- member path, same as M.*(x, y)
f| x                 -- named prefix operator, same arity as f(x)
x |f                 -- named postfix operator, same arity as f(x)

(.x + y.)            -- source grouping that renders as x + y
(x + y)[:sum:]       -- labeled expression

(x_ is \\real) => x_ + 1      -- function literal (anonymous function value)
(? is \\real) -> (? is \\real) -- function type
? is \\real                   -- spec literal (a value of \\\\specification)
x satisfies (? is \\real)     -- apply a spec: means x is \\real
\\function:from{A?}:to{B?}    -- X? declares X inline with the required type
f[|value = x, ... = 0|]      -- named function call with a catch-all
```

Any sequence of special-operator characters can be an operator, and an operator may carry trailing primes and a subscript, as in `*'`, `*_1`, and `*'_a`. Names may carry prime marks too (`X'`, `x'_a'`), and `` `*` `` is the operator `*` referred to by name. Equality and inequality, `=` and `!=`, are accepted for any types even when a type-specific capability is not defined.

The command sugar `\\foo[x_ is \\real]{x_ + 1}` expands to `\\foo{(x_ is \\real) => x_ + 1}`, and `\\foo{x_ : ...}` expands to the single collection argument `\\foo{{x_ : ...}}`."
Id: "7b1d159f-93e3-4658-8ddd-8963da177135"

SectionTitle: "Declarations And Statements"
Id: "58e2e50d-b352-4f64-8704-645607155f4f"

Text: "```text
X is \\set
X, Y is \\set
X is! \\set                          -- hard view (coercion + encoding)
G is \\set via X
G ::= (X, *, e)                     -- destructuring; introduces X, *, e
X ::= x(i_) ::= y_                  -- mapping alias with an output placeholder
X := \\set@{x_ : x_ is \\real}
f(x__) := x__ is \\function:on{A}:to{A}
x is? \\set
x is_not? \\set
```

`::=` introduces structural shape and its nested symbols; `:=` introduces a value definition and its right-hand side must already be in scope; `=` is mathematical equality."
Id: "0871023c-b4d5-42dd-b39d-576780a6d935"

SectionTitle: "Collection Expressions"
Id: "4570669c-633d-45d1-8bc1-5ebe274916ce"

Text: "```text
{x_ : x_ is \\set}
{x_ : x_ is \\set | x_ != \\empty.set}
{(p_, q_) : ...}
{\\equivalence.class:of{x_}:over{R} : x_ \"in\" X}
{z_ ::= (a_, b_) : a_ \"in\" A; b_ \"in\" B | z_ := \\ordered.pair:of{a_}:and{b_}}
```

The `: ...` part contains specifications, separated by commas or semicolons. The optional `| ...` part contains a predicate expression or a value binding such as `z_ := ...`."
Id: "cbca77f6-b13e-467a-8d43-139bb851ad54"

SectionTitle: "Top-Level Items"
Id: "be3049e5-0942-4b6b-af8e-1b587c988ab6"

Text: "Every top-level item must have an `Id:`. `mlg check` generates missing UUID v4 IDs before checking and reports duplicate IDs.

```text
[\\set]
Defines: X
Requires:
. capability: x_ \"in\" X :-> \\\\abstract
  written: \"x_? \\in X?\"
Documented:
. called: \"set\"
Id: \"059126b9-dc83-41a2-aa1c-84f8e942f8d6\"
```

`Defines:`, `Declares:`, and `States:` require at least one of `called:` or `written:` in `Documented:`. Missing `called:` or `written:` rendering is generated from the one that is present. `Refines:` requires `adjective:` and may have `written:`, but may not have `called:`.

The full set of top-level items is `Defines:`, `Declares:`, `Refines:`, `States:`, `Disambiguates:`, `Axiom:`, `Theorem:`, `Conjecture:`, `Relation:` (a bidirectional relationship between two concepts, topics, or definitions), `Equivalent:` (an equivalence class of interchangeable notations), `Topic:` (a documentation topic, `[#some.name]`), `Specify:`, `Person:`, `Resource:`, `Writing:`, the opaque placeholders `TextTheorem:`, `TextAxiom:`, `TextConjecture:`, and `TextDefinition:`, and the page-content items `Title:`, `SectionTitle:`, `SubsectionTitle:`, and `Text:`.

The support sections of a definition-like item appear in this order: `Requires:`, `Enables:`, `Documented:`, `Justification:`, `Aliases:`, `Writing:`, `References:`, `Metadata:`, `Id:`."
Id: "37001eb1-5f07-44ba-a2eb-7fd297edacea"

SectionTitle: "Requires And Enables"
Id: "9ba6a690-a9bd-4d6b-8bef-b8662a760142"

Text: "`Requires:` describes capabilities or definitions that are part of the construct. `Enables:` describes additional capabilities, build sources, and relations.

```text
Requires:
. capability: x_ \"in\" X :-> x_ member_of X
  written: \"x_? \\in X?\"
. definition: \\natural.0 is \\natural

Enables:
. capability: x_ - y_ :=> x_ \\.set.minus./ y_
. from: Y ::= {y__ : ...}
  capability: x_ \"in\" X :-> x_ member_of Y
. from: P ::= {(p_, q_) : ...}
  as: f(p_) := q_
. relation:
  to: r := \\rational@n is \\rational
  when: n is \\integer
  means: n \\.embedded.to./ r
  represents: \\\\coercion
```

The checker unions capabilities from `Requires:` and `Enables:` when resolving notation. A `Requires:` `definition:` must name a `Declares:` entry. A nested `Enables: relation:` backs builds: `represents: \\\\coercion` allows a soft `\\type@value` build; `represents: \\\\encoding` additionally allows a hard `\\type@!value` build."
Id: "685a6713-cb6d-4c0a-887a-aa484a20223b"

SectionTitle: "Clauses"
Id: "a777d902-4589-4031-b919-fe4964632e64"

Text: "```text
allOf:
. P
. Q

anyOf:
. P
. Q

exists: x is \\real

existsUnique: x is \\real
suchThat: x > 0

forAll: x \"in\" X
then: x is? \\set

let: n \"in\" X
where: n != x
then: n = n

if: P
then: Q

have:
. P
iff: Q

have:
. P
asserting:
. Q
```

`exists:` and `existsUnique:` do not require `suchThat:`. The old `iff: ... then: ...` group is not supported; use `have: ... iff: ...`. A `have:` group is dispatched by its second section label, so `asserting:` makes it the assertion group rather than the biconditional."
Id: "d21dd513-1286-4a0a-878a-128234ac5cbe"

SectionTitle: "Writing"
Id: "0f6dbb65-26a0-42e0-b3df-99da0f727069"

Text: "A collection may contain one top-level `Writing:` block, and every non-prose top-level item may carry its own.

```text
Writing:
. \"alpha :~> \\alpha\"
. \"beta :~> \\beta\"
```

Each alias is a **double-quoted** string; the left side must be a plain name and the body after `:~>` is raw LaTeX. The renderer applies writing aliases to names in expressions, statements, and specifications, including underscore parts and numeric suffixes: `alpha_1` renders as `\\alpha_1`, and `alpha123` renders as `\\alpha_{123}`."
Id: "29643f55-8a3c-4e5a-9e95-6ae64799787a"

SectionTitle: "People And Resources"
Id: "7b2968f1-4319-4bde-9569-1f46caf6233a"

Text: "```text
[@ada.lovelace]
Person: \"Ada Lovelace\"
biography: \"Markdown biography text.\"
```

`biography:` is optional. Resource cards use their `title:` as the card title and hyperlink it when `url:` is present; the URL itself is shown in the hidden details accordion. A `References:` entry names a resource with `$book.chapter`, and a PDF reference may append `:page{n}`."
Id: "41e2fbba-15f0-447a-bb4d-36b8a2a8467f"
